<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lapita pottery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lapita pottery is the dentate-stamped earthenware that defines the Lapita culture of the western Pacific, which appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> about 1500 BCE. Low-fired and tempered with shell or sand, it bears fine geometric patterns pressed into the clay with toothed stamps and closely resembles pottery from Nagsabaran in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. First found on Watom Island in 1909 and named after a site in New Caledonia excavated in 1952, it is known from more than 200 sites between the Bismarck Archipelago and Samoa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lapita-pottery.jpg" alt="Lapita pottery" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torbenbrinker · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PortVilaLapita.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
